1
00:00:00,824 --> 00:00:09,137
Welcome to South America episode three of three. First, I clear the chosen
names for a fresh regional lesson.

2
00:00:09,792 --> 00:00:20,926
Finally the east and the south: Brazil filling the eastern half of the
continent, with Paraguay, Uruguay and Argentina below it.

3
00:00:21,593 --> 00:00:26,156
Let's bring Brazil and its neighbours into view.

4
00:00:28,372 --> 00:00:40,865
Brazil occupies the east of the continent, with the Atlantic along its coast,
and every other South American country except Chile and Ecuador touching it.
The Amazon crosses its north.

5
00:00:41,522 --> 00:01:01,142
Its name comes from brazilwood, a tree whose red dye the Portuguese traded,
and the tree's own name comes from brasa, "ember". It is the only country in
the Americas whose main language is Portuguese, and the Amazon carries about a
fifth of all the river water that reaches the oceans.

6
00:01:01,794 --> 00:01:09,422
Remember Brazil as the giant on the east: the only Portuguese-speaking country
in the Americas, with the Amazon across its north.

7
00:01:10,091 --> 00:01:13,795
Let's bring Paraguay and its neighbours into view.

8
00:01:16,014 --> 00:01:26,033
Paraguay is landlocked between Bolivia to the north, Brazil to the east and
Argentina to the south and west. The Paraguay river runs through its middle.

9
00:01:26,688 --> 00:01:45,084
It is named for the Paraguay river, a Guaraní name whose meaning is disputed.
Most of its people speak Guaraní as well as Spanish, and both are official.
The Itaipú dam on its border with Brazil is one of the largest hydroelectric
plants in the world.

10
00:01:45,746 --> 00:01:53,757
Remember Paraguay as the country between the river and the great neighbours,
with two official languages, one of them Guaraní.

11
00:01:54,438 --> 00:01:58,014
Let's bring Uruguay and its neighbours into view.

12
00:02:00,209 --> 00:02:11,297
Uruguay is the smallest country of the southern group, on the Atlantic between
Argentina to its west and Brazil to its north, across the wide mouth of the
Uruguay river.

13
00:02:11,956 --> 00:02:28,912
It is named for that river, a Guaraní name whose meaning is disputed. It
hosted and won the first football World Cup, in 1930. In 2013 it became the
first country to make cannabis fully legal.

14
00:02:29,575 --> 00:02:39,850
Remember Uruguay as the small Atlantic country that hosted the first World
Cup, in 1930, and changed its cannabis law in 2013.

15
00:02:40,536 --> 00:02:44,205
Let's bring Argentina and its neighbours into view.

16
00:02:46,406 --> 00:03:00,698
Argentina fills the southern part of the continent, with Chile to its west,
Paraguay and Bolivia to the north, Brazil to the north-east and Uruguay to the
east. Tierra del Fuego lies at its far south.

17
00:03:01,356 --> 00:03:21,624
Its name comes from Latin argentum, "silver", for the silver the Spanish hoped
to find up the Río de la Plata. Ushuaia, on Tierra del Fuego, calls itself the
southernmost city in the world. The tango was born in the port districts of
Buenos Aires and Montevideo in the late 19th century.

18
00:03:22,288 --> 00:03:32,261
Remember Argentina as the great southern country named for silver that was
never found, with Aconcagua, the tango and the southernmost city at its end.

19
00:03:36,141 --> 00:03:49,493
Brazil touches every other South American country except Chile and Ecuador.
Paraguay is landlocked between Brazil and Argentina, Uruguay is on the
Atlantic, and Argentina fills the far south.

20
00:03:50,145 --> 00:04:01,360
Remember brazilwood and Portuguese and the Amazon, two official languages and
Itaipú, the first World Cup in 1930, and the silver that gave Argentina its
name.
